# Rust Clean Code & Optimization (NEXUS)

Always follow these two sources when writing or reviewing Rust in this repo:

1. **Clean code** — https://dev.to/mbayoun95/rust-clean-code-crafting-elegant-efficient-and-maintainable-software-27ce
2. **Optimization** — https://gist.github.com/jFransham/369a86eff00e5f280ed25121454acec1

## Clean code (always)

- Meaningful names; no cryptic `x` / `f` / `tmp` for domain values.
- Single responsibility: each function does one thing; split complex flows.
- Prefer simplicity — no premature abstractions or unused params “for later” unless the next slice already needs them.
- Model domain with `enum` / `struct` / traits instead of magic ints/strings.
- Errors via `Result` / `Option`; no `panic!` for expected failure paths.
- Comments explain **why**, not what; use `///` on public APIs.
- Prefer borrowing (`&T`, `&str`, `&[T]`) over unnecessary owned clones.
- Prefer `match` and idiomatic `Option`/`Result` combinators over noisy `if`/`unwrap`.
- After edits: run `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy`; keep tests green with `cargo test`.

## Optimization (profile before cleverness)

From jFransham — apply in this order:

1. **Don't optimize blindly** — readability first unless a real hotspot exists.
2. **Never optimize without evidence** — prefer algorithm fixes and `clippy` over micro-hacks.
3. **Skip one-shot costs** — hot paths (REPL read/lex/parse/exec) matter more than startup chrome.
4. **Prefer better algorithms / fewer passes** — avoid extra `collect()` loops when one pass works.
5. **Reduce allocations on hot paths** — reuse buffers (`String`/`Vec` cleared in place); prefer stack/`arrayvec`-style when sizes are bounded; avoid `Box<dyn Trait>` in inner loops.
6. **Keep hot data compact and contiguous** — cache-friendly layouts for ASTs/tokens later.
7. **No `#[inline(always)]` by default**; consider LTO only when measuring release builds.

## NEXUS-specific mapping

- Shell command language follows Dragon Book phases: **lex → parse → IR/exec**.
- REPL I/O must stay allocation-light (reuse the line buffer).
- Minishell errors go to **stderr**; program failure exit code **84** (Epitech convention).
